# WN22-SO-000430: Windows Server 2022 User Account Control (UAC) must only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations.
# Source: Windows Server 2022 STIG r8
# Severity: medium
# Generated from 01-extract/output/windows-server-2022-stig-r8-controls.json

META
    esp_id `win2022-wn22-so-000430-001`
    version `1.0.0`
    dsl_schema_version `1.0.0`
    platform `windows`
    criticality `high`

    title `Windows Server 2022 User Account Control (UAC) must only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations.`
    description `UAC is a security mechanism for limiting the elevation of privileges, including administrative accounts, unless authorized. This setting configures Windows to only allow applications installed in a secure location on the file system, such as the Program Files or the Windows\System32 folders, to run with elevated privileges.`
    author `scanset-security`
    tags `windows,server-2022,stig,registry,medium`

    control_mapping `NIST-800-53:SC-3`
    control_objective `WN22-SO-000430`

    assessment_method `TEST`
    implementation_status `implemented`
    responsible_role `system-admin`

    control_origination `sp-system`
    inherited_from `Windows:server-2022:endpoint`
    customer_responsibility `configuration`

    remediation_days `30`
    recommendation `Configure the policy value for Computer Configuration >> Windows Settings >> Security Settings >> Local Policies >> Security Options >> User Account Control: Only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations to "Enabled".`

    component_id `comp-windows-registry`

    impl_SC_3 `Windows Server implements SC-3 by setting registry value 'EnableSecureUIAPaths' under 'HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System' to the STIG-mandated REG_DWORD value. The ESP scanner reads the value via the registry CTN and fails the policy if the key is absent or the value does not match the required setting. Remediation restores the configuration via Group Policy or direct registry edit per the STIG fix guidance.`

META_END

DEF
    OBJECT target
        hive `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE`
        key `SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\System`
        name `EnableSecureUIAPaths`
    OBJECT_END

    STATE compliant
        exists boolean = true
        value_int int = 1
    STATE_END

    CRI AND
        CTN registry
            TEST all all AND
            STATE_REF compliant
            OBJECT_REF target
        CTN_END
    CRI_END
DEF_END
